  "nameStory": "The hairy-banded mining bee gets its name from the distinctive bands of fuzzy hair that wrap around its abdomen like tiny belts. These 'bands' are actually dense patches of branched hairs called plumose hairs that help the bee collect pollen. The 'mining' part comes from their nesting behavior - females dig burrows in the ground like tiny miners excavating underground tunnels.",
  "description": "The hairy-banded mining bee is a medium-sized solitary bee that builds its nest by digging tunnels in bare or sparsely vegetated soil. Unlike honeybees that live in colonies, these bees work alone, with each female creating her own underground burrow. They're important early-season pollinators, often seen visiting spring wildflowers and fruit trees. These bees are about the size of a small honeybee, with a fuzzy body marked by distinct bands of hair across their abdomen.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Hairy bands on abdomen",
      "description": "Look for distinct bands of dense, light-colored hair wrapped around the abdomen segments - these feel soft and appear almost fluffy when viewed closely",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Ground-nesting behavior",
      "description": "Watch for bees flying low to the ground and disappearing into small holes in bare soil - you might notice tiny piles of excavated dirt around entrance holes",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Body size and shape",
      "description": "Similar in size to a honeybee but with a more robust, fuzzy appearance and less distinct waist between thorax and abdomen",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Flight pattern",
      "description": "Flies with a slightly buzzy, purposeful flight pattern, often hovering briefly over potential nesting sites before landing",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Pollen-carrying behavior",
      "description": "Females carry dry pollen on their leg hairs and belly rather than in neat pollen baskets like honeybees - the pollen appears dusty and loose",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Look for hairy-banded mining bees in areas with well-drained, sandy or loamy soil that receives good sun exposure. They prefer sites with sparse vegetation like path edges, garden borders, south-facing slopes, and disturbed ground near woodlands. You'll often find their nesting burrows in the same areas where spring wildflowers bloom, as females need nearby nectar sources.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "Peak activity time when adults emerge and begin nesting. Look for females digging burrows and visiting early flowers like spring beauty, trout lily, and fruit tree blossoms.",
    "summer": "Adult activity winds down by early summer as the current generation completes reproduction. Larvae develop underground in individual cells stocked with pollen.",
    "fall": "No above-ground activity visible. New generation develops as larvae in underground cells, feeding on stored pollen provisions.",
    "winter": "Adults overwinter as fully-formed bees in underground cells, remaining dormant until spring warmth triggers emergence."
  },
  "funFact": "Each female hairy-banded mining bee is essentially a single mom who creates an entire underground apartment complex - she digs a main tunnel up to 8 inches deep with multiple side chambers, stocks each room with a ball of pollen and nectar, lays a single egg on top, then seals it shut like a time capsule for her offspring to find when they hatch.",
  "confusionNotes": "Most commonly confused with other mining bee species in the Andrena genus, but the distinct hairy bands on the abdomen are the key distinguishing feature. Memory trick: 'Hairy bands = hairy-banded' - if you can clearly see fuzzy rings around the belly, you've likely found your bee.",
  "contextualKnowledge": "Despite being called 'mining bees,' these insects are beneficial pollinators, not destructive pests. Their ground-nesting behavior sometimes concerns gardeners who mistake the small soil holes for damage, but these bees are actually helping pollinate nearby plants and generally don't sting unless directly handled.",
  "compareHints": "Among New England's many Andrena species, hairy-banded mining bees are distinguished by their prominent abdominal hair bands, which are more distinct than the subtle markings found on species like the cloudy-winged mining bee or spring beauty miner. While other mining bees may have fuzzy bodies, few have such clearly defined bands that look like tiny belts wrapped around their middle.",
